  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot and humid conditions with heat indices \naround 105F expected through the rest of the week. Heat Advisories \nhave been issued for inland locations for today and tomorrow. \n\nA late summer (or early fall, depending on what definition you \nprefer) heat wave remains on tap for the rest of the week. The heat \nshould peak today and tomorrow as a strong UL ridge builds, its axis \njust to the west of the local area. There is increased confidence in \na backdoor cold front at the sfc will move in, leading to winds \nbecoming NE and providing a little bit of relative relief from the \nheat near the coast. Inland temps peak in the mid to upper 90s and \nheat indices between 102 and 106 while coastal areas stay in the 80s \nand low 90s. After collaboration with neighbors, the decision was \nmade to go ahead and expand the Heat Advisory for today to include \nour piedmont counties. The heat continues into tomorrow with higher \nconfidence in widespread heat indices at or exceeding 105. This time \nthe coastal counties will be included in the party since winds will \nshift back to the SW. Went ahead and extended today's heat \nadvisories through tomorrow since confidence is high. Advisories for \nthe coastal counties will likely be added in following forecast \npackages. The heat continues Friday, but a cold front passage could \nmean some relief, especially across northern counties. The extent of \npotential heat advisories will depend on exact timing of the front, \nbut there is a pretty good chance that at least NE NC and SE VA get \nanother round of them. We will finally see a break from the hot \nweather behind the front over the weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There are daily chances for thunderstorms \nthrough Saturday. Some storms may be strong to severe with damaging \nwinds gusts and large hail.  \n\nUnfortunately the confidence in the thunderstorm forecast is not as \nhigh as the confidence in the temperature forecast. Today has the \nlowest confidence in severe weather of the next several days \nwith a wide variety of solutions from the CAMs. It is one of \nthose days where the ceiling for potential impacts is high, but \nthe confidence in storm coverage is rather low. Still, if storms\ncan form near the local area there is chance that they just \nride the boundary (backdoor front) south and sweep through with \ndamaging wind gusts and perhaps hail. Some of the more typically\naggressive CAMs, such at the NAM3k, depict this very scenario \nand have an MCS shooting down the I-95 corridor. SPC has \noutlooked the northern half of the area with a Slight clipping \nthe NE and a marginal down to roughly the US-460 corridor. \n\nThe strong ridge aloft starts to break down/flatten late in the \nweek. Meanwhile, a sfc cold front will approach and pass through the \narea sometime Friday, likely late. An initial round of storms will \nbe possible Thursday, but coverage still looks on the low side and \nthese chances ultimately depend on where that backdoor front is \nsituated. The most favorable overlap of forcing, shear, and \ninstability is expected Friday. At the surface, the aforementioned \nfront to our N will finally make appreciable progress and descend \ninto our local area. Aloft, NW flow will remain in place and \nprogressively strengthen as the associated trough drops southward \nalong the NE/Mid-Atlantic coast, increasing the deep-layer shear. \nThis all suggests a bonafide severe wx threat for the majority of \nthe area Friday, with the potential for several fast-moving clusters \ncapable of widespread damaging winds, along with isolated supercells \nand large hail. SPC has expanded the Slight Risk for Thursday to \nencompass most of the area, and a similar outlook is anticipated in \nthe updated Day 3 outlook for Friday. The risk for storms (and any \npotential svr wx) Saturday is dependent on where the front is \nsituated during peak heating.",
